Contributors whose ideas and work shaped this fork
--------------------------------------------------
Nikhil-42 — https://github.com/Nikhil-42
    Proposed unencrypted-backup support, an "Extract Entire Backup" function and
    mounting a backup as a file system, in upstream BackupLens pull requests #1
    and #2. Their designs inspired the matching features here, which are
    independent implementations for this project's structure.
jakubstetz — https://github.com/jakubstetz
    Upstream pull request #5 found and fixed the same encrypted-extraction,
    threading and macOS startup bugs.
heebeejeebees — https://github.com/heebeejeebees
    Asked for unencrypted-backup support (upstream issue #4).

Upstream components credited
----------------------------
James Sharkey, Jean-Baptiste Bedrune and Jean Sigwald — iphone_backup_decrypt
and the iphone-dataprotection code it builds on. See NOTICE.

Projects and research whose published work this one learned from
----------------------------------------------------------------
No code was copied from these. The Keychain tab, the opening of locked notes
and of the files in clinical records, and the reading of Health records were
written independently, from what these projects and documents publish
about the formats; they are credited here, and in NOTICE and README, because
the work would not have been possible without them.

iphone-dataprotection — Jean-Baptiste Bedrune and Jean Sigwald (Sogeti ESEC
Lab). The layout of the keychain items in an encrypted backup (version 3: a key
wrapped with the class key, AES-GCM with an empty nonce, DER-encoded
attributes) and the protection classes.
    https://github.com/dinosec/iphone-dataprotection
Apple Cloud Notes Parser — Jon Baumann, Ciofeca Forensics (threeplanetssoftware). Where a locked
note and its attachments keep their lock details (salt, rounds, wrapped key,
nonce, tag) and how they are opened.
    https://github.com/threeplanetssoftware/apple_cloud_notes_parser
hashcat — the hashcat developers. Its Apple Secure Notes module (hash mode
16200) confirms how a locked note's key is derived and unwrapped.
    https://hashcat.net/hashcat/
Apple Platform Security Guide — Apple Inc. The documented design of locked
notes and of keychain data protection.
    https://support.apple.com/guide/security/
RFC 3394 (AES key wrap) and NIST SP 800-38D (AES-GCM) — the standards the
cryptography follows, and the source of the test vectors.
PyCryptodome — https://www.pycryptodome.org — the AES implementation used.
kinnay/AEA (python-aea) — Yannik Marchand (MIT License). A Python implementation
of the Apple Encrypted Archive format, against which the reader for the files in
Health's clinical records was checked (byte for byte on real files) and from
which one test archive was made.
    https://github.com/kinnay/AEA
ipsw — blacktop (MIT License). Its AEA decoder, written down in prose, describes
the key derivation steps of the format.
    https://github.com/blacktop/ipsw
The Apple Wiki — its page on the Apple Encrypted Archive format (profiles, header
and layout). https://theapplewiki.com/wiki/Apple_Encrypted_Archive
Protocol Buffers encoding guide — Google (https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/encoding/),
the public description of the wire format that the Maps records of iOS 12 use.
HL7 FHIR — the open standard (https://hl7.org/fhir) in which Health stores the
records it receives from clinics; reading them relies on its published names.
Apple HealthKit documentation — Apple Inc. The public names and values of
sleep stages, category samples (symptoms, cycle tracking, events) and clinical
record types.
